  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 214 AM CDT Wed Sep 30 2026\n\nThe main change with this forecast package is to shift the axis of \nheaviest forecast precipitation over the next 48 hours further \nsouthward. Consequently, the flood watch has been extended one to \ntwo counties south and eastward to cover most the area along and \nnorth of the Highway 36 corridor. It is in this area that 2-5\" of \nprecipitation is forecast. This flood watch will be in effect \nthrough 1am Friday morning.\n\nEarly this morning, moisture continues to stream northward into the \narea on a 40-45kt southwesterly LLJ. This is continuing shower and \nisolated thunderstorm chances across the area overnight. The LLJ \nnever weakens during the course of the day today and continues to \nnose into the area which will continue shower and thunderstorm \nchances through the day today. Additional lift will be introduced \nthis afternoon as a couple lead upper level shortwaves will eject \nout ahead of a closed upper level low which will slowly move through \nthe desert southwest today. This will provide for the better chance \nfor some thunderstorms this afternoon as well as the potential for a \ncouple rounds of heavy rainfall. PWATs this afternoon into this \nevening will range between 2.00\"2.25\" which is near the 100th \npercentile for late Sept/early October. Tonight, the closed low over \nthe desert southwest will move out into the western Plains forcing a \ncold front towards he area. The persistent LLJ coupled with \nadditional lead upper level shortwaves will continue the potential \nfor addition heavy showers and storms. Tomorrow the closed upper \nlevel low will move slowly across Kansas and force the cold front \nslowly through the area during the day Thursday. PWATs will still \nremain in the 1.75-2.00\" range continue the chances for heavy rain. \nTomorrow night the upper level low finally opens up becoming a more \nprogressive wave as it moves through the region. This will bring one \nlast shot of precipitation to the area before he upper level system \nshifts east by Friday morning. All told areas along and north of \nHighway 36 are forecast to receive 2 to 4+ inches of precipitation \nwith 1 to 3 inches forecast further south. Highs today will be in \nthe mid 70s to mid 80s but will be cooler on Thursday as the cold \nfront moves through with highs ranging in the upper 60s to upper 70s.\n\nFortunately, conditions will dry out Friday and then look dry \nthrough the rest of the forecast period. Friday and Saturday a \nsurface ridge of high pressure will dominate the area providing \nearly fall-like temperatures with highs in the mid 60s to mid 70s. \nSunday a upper level ridge of high pressure over the southwestern \nCONUS will lowly shift eastward into the western High Plains. This \nwill provide gradual height rises across the area allowing highs to \nrise a few degrees into the upper 60s to mid 70s. Monday a surface \nridge will build into the area from the northern Plains keeping \nhighs in the lower 60s to lower 70s. Tuesday the surface ridge of \nhigh pressure shifts east of the area with modest WAA developing and \nallowing highs to rise into the mid 60s to mid 70s.",
